      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8026-a3a9-cc797b4e9916",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Dependency Injection (DI)",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " is a design pattern where objects (called dependencies) are passed into a class instead of the class creating them itself."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80d6-8ab1-d024ffdb6a80",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "In Spring Boot, DI makes your code "
        ],
        [
          "loosely coupled",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", "
        ],
        [
          "easier to test",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", and "
        ],
        [
          "easier to maintain",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ". Spring Boot automates this using annotations like "
        ],
        [
          "@Autowired",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ", so you don't have to write the wiring code manually."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80c5-8551-f9e2a9b1f4ff",
      "type": "text",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Think of it like this (C++ analogy)",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          ": Instead of a class using "
        ],
        [
          "new",
          [
            [
              "c"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          " to create its own objects, someone else gives it the ready-made object. That “someone else” in Spring is the "
        ],
        [
          "Spring Container",
          [
            [
              "b"
            ]
          ]
        ],
        [
          "."
        ]
