    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-800b-9c97-d019aa419c08",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "❓ "
        ],
        [
          "What is Dependency Injection and how does Spring Boot handle it?",
          [
            [
              "i"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8015-aab7-fa7f960c1311",
      "type": "quote",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Dependency Injection is a design pattern where dependencies are passed into a class instead of being created inside it. Spring Boot simplifies this using annotations like @Autowired, which tells the framework to automatically inject the required dependencies. This makes the code more modular, testable, and maintainable."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-807f-b0c8-d6f02ce9ab36",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "❓ "
        ],
        [
          "What is the difference between @Component, @Service, and @Repository?",
          [
            [
              "i"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8080-b789-f137f6e1aa79",
      "type": "quote",
      "richText": [
        [
          "All three are Spring stereotypes that mark a class as a Spring-managed bean. @Component is generic, while @Service is used for business logic, and @Repository is for database interaction. They help organize the code and enable additional Spring features like exception translation in repositories."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80e6-bf40-dfb565d45a32",
      "type": "sub_sub_header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "❓ "
        ],
        [
          "Why should you prefer constructor injection over field injection?",
          [
            [
              "i"
            ]
          ]
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-80c0-bf40-cae82fbd8347",
      "type": "quote",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Constructor injection promotes immutability and makes your classes easier to test because dependencies must be provided when creating the object. Field injection hides the dependencies and makes unit testing more difficult."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "20824eb1-ed54-8073-9167-d949718b4b63",
      "type": "divider"
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-stereotype-choice",
      "type": "header",
      "richText": [
        [
          "Choose a stereotype by responsibility"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-stereotype-choice-b0",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "@Component registers a general component. @Service communicates that a bean holds application or business operations; it does not automatically create a transaction."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-stereotype-choice-b1",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "@Repository identifies data access and participates in Spring’s persistence-exception translation when the relevant infrastructure is configured."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-stereotype-choice-b2",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "These stereotypes do not force a folder structure. Scanning and bean registration determine whether the class is available for injection."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ed-stereotype-choice-b3",
      "type": "bulleted_list",
      "richText": [
        [
          "When two beans implement the same interface, select intentionally with @Qualifier or a suitable @Primary. Renaming a local variable is not a reliable dependency-selection strategy."
        ]
      ]
    }
